{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>This month.</b> Like this one — see it and sanctify it. And everything depends on the sanctification of the court, as Rabbi Oshaya says: When Israel arrive at Rosh HaShanah, the Holy One, blessed be He, says to the ministering angels: Set up the scale and bring out the books, for tomorrow I come and judge My world. In the meantime, the court below deliberates and declares and postpones Rosh HaShanah to the following day. The next day, the Holy One, blessed be He, does not judge His world — the ministering angels stand before the Holy One, blessed be He, and say to Him: Master of the universe, did You not say to us, 'Tomorrow I judge My world' — and the judgment has slipped away? He says to them: My children declare and postpone the judgment until tomorrow, and the matter is in their hands, for you must follow their determination, and whatever they do is done along with Him. And on this Moses, peace be upon him, said: ״כי מי גוי גדול אשר לו״ etc. — ״בכל קראנו אליו״ — read it: our calling to Him, i.e., our calling of the new moons and the festivals to Him, they too are called and made. And the Sages said that this is one of the three things that Moses, peace be upon him, found difficult. And there is a difficulty: for the moon is small at the end of the month just as at the beginning of the molad, so how can one distinguish whether it is from the old or from the new? The answer: even so, one can distinguish from the tilt of its crescent, for the old moon's tilt is toward the west and the new one's toward the east. Another interpretation: at the end [of its cycle] it stands in the southeast corner, and at its renewal in the southwest corner."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:02:59.101803423Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}